WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.100
Look at this set of numbers.

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:11.420
Twelve, sixteen, twelve, six, eighteen, two and four.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.780
Let's find out the arithmetic mean of these numbers first.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.680
We know that it equals the sum of values

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.180
divided by the number of values.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:25.620
The sum of values would be 12 plus 16 plus 12 plus

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:29.360
6 plus 18 plus 2 plus 4.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:34.540
And there are 1, 2, 3 ,4, 5, 6 &amp; 7.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.410
7 values.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.600
We divide this sum by 7.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.460
12 plus 16 is 28,

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.320
28 plus 12 is 40,

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.780
40 plus 6 is 46,

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:51.120
46 plus 18 is 64,

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.760
and 64 plus 6 is 70.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:58.860
So the mean would equal 70 divided by 7.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:01.200
That gives us 10.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.800
So the mean of these seven numbers is 10.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:08.740
What we are basically doing is finding an approximate central value

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.140
of a given set of values.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:15.260
So there are a couple of other ways to find the central value.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:16.800
To understand them,

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.440
we first need to arrange the values in ascending order,

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:23.620
from the least number to the highest number.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.640
'2' as we can see is the least number.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.780
We write a '2' first.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.300
The next higher number is '4'.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.260
We write a '4' next.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:34.920
Then '6',

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.320
it is always a good idea to mark the numbers you have written

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:39.940
with a pencil.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:41.540
If the list is huge,

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:44.920
there is a chance that you might miss out a few numbers.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.920
So '6' would come next

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:50.540
the next highest number after '6' is '12'.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.160
And it occurs twice.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.520
So we write '12' and '12' here.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.280
Then '16' then '18'.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:03.260
We write '16' and '18' and that completes our list.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.360
Make sure you have covered all values.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:08.360
Arranging it in an ascending order

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:11.040
gives us something called the 'median'.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.000
The median is the middle value of the list

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:17.360
when it's arranged in ascending order.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.000
There are seven values we have here.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:23.580
And we can see that '12' is the middle value.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.360
So the median of this set of values is '12'.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:32.100
The median is just another way to measure the central tendency.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.000
So the mean is the average,

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.560
and the median is the middle value.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:40.880
But only after we have arranged the terms in ascending order.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.620
And the third concept is that of a mode.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:48.620
It is the value that occurs more frequently.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.660
In our list we can see that '12' occurs twice.

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:55.960
And all other numbers occur just once.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.860
So the mode of this set of values is '12'.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:03.520
Why do we have three concepts to know the central tendency ?

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:05.940
Isn't one enough?

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.460
What are the applications of each?

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:13.720
All these questions will be answered in the coming videos.

